<section class="content-section" id="guidelines-intro">
    <div class="content-wrapper">

        <p>
            Words of Plainness is a space where seekers and saints find common ground. That means
            people arrive here from very different places: different faith traditions, different
            levels of conviction, different wounds, different questions. Some are lifelong disciples.
            Some are skeptics trying to be honest with themselves. Most are somewhere in between.
        </p>
        <p>
            What makes this community work is not agreement. It is the commitment to treat every
            person with the dignity that is theirs as a child of God, regardless of where they
            are in their journey.
        </p>
        <p>
            Words of Plainness is a ministry of fellowship for disciples of Jesus Christ of every
            tradition, and for all who are still searching for Him. By participating in the Words
            of Plainness community, we agree to abide by the following expectations for a safe
            common ground in which to learn, to bear witness of God, to socialize, and to
            understand the faith of others. The boundaries we agree to uphold make honest
            interfaith conversation possible.
        </p>
        <p>
            This agreement applies to every gathering this ministry hosts: in-person gatherings,
            live VR chat sessions, this website, our Discord community, and any other venues or
            platforms we choose. It is not an aspirational suggestion. It is the terms of
            participation.
        </p>

        <div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--gold-primary);">
            <p style="margin: 0; font-style: italic;">
                “Where Seekers and Saints Find Common Ground”: that is our tagline and our
                commitment. Common ground requires that everyone be safe on it.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- THEOLOGICAL FOUNDATION -->
<section class="content-section" id="guidelines-foundation">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>The Theological Foundation of This Community</h2>
        <p>
            This community exists because Christ’s reach is boundless. The writer of Hebrews
            declared that He is “able to save them to the uttermost that come unto God by him”
            (Hebrews 7:25), which leaves no room for the assumption that any honest seeker is
            beyond His grace or outside the scope of this ministry. Paul confirmed the same truth
            in language that crossed every boundary of his world: “There is neither Jew nor Greek,
            there is neither bond nor free, there is neither male nor female: for ye are all one
            in Christ Jesus” (Galatians 3:28). And God said it plainly to Peter: “God is no
            respecter of persons: but in every nation he that feareth him, and worketh
            righteousness, is accepted with him” (Acts 10:34-35).
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>These are not aspirational statements.</strong> They are the doctrinal ground
            on which every decision in this community is made, including the decision to remove
            someone from it.
        </p>
        <p>
            The corollary is equally plain. If Christ receives all who come to Him, then any
            person who appoints themselves a guardian of His grace, who excludes, condemns, or
            intimidates another seeker, has set themselves against the very purpose of this
            ministry. The Spirit of Christ does not coexist with the spirit of contention: “He
            that hath the spirit of contention is not of me, but is of the devil, who is the
            father of contention” (3 Nephi 11:29). “The servant of the Lord must not strive; but
            be gentle unto all men, apt to teach, patient” (2 Timothy 2:24).
        </p>
        <p>
            When someone persists in divisive behavior after clear correction, scripture is
            equally direct: “A man that is an heretick after the first and second admonition
            reject; knowing that he that is such is subverted, and sinneth, being condemned of
            himself” (Titus 3:10-11). The word Paul uses for “heretick” describes a factious or
            divisive person, not someone who simply believes differently.
        </p>
        <p>
            This is not a punitive policy. It is a pastoral one. The community cannot be a safe
            place for the seeker if it is also a platform for those determined to drive seekers
            away. The agreement below is the practical expression of these convictions. It is
            enforced with that foundation in view, not to protect the institution, but to protect
            the people inside it.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- OUR AGREEMENT -->
<section class="content-section alt" id="guidelines-rules">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>Our Agreement</h2>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">What we ask of one another</h3>
        <ol style="margin: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1.25rem; line-height: 1.9;">
            <li><strong>Honor one another’s devotion.</strong> Christians in our gatherings hold
                irreconcilable convictions about baptism, priesthood authority, sacraments,
                revelation, and ecclesiastical authority. We ask each participant to honor the
                sincere devotion to Jesus Christ of those who differ from them.</li>
            <li><strong>Disagree with respect.</strong> You may disagree with an idea. You may not
                hold a person in contempt for holding it. Critique arguments. Ask hard questions.
                Challenge assumptions. Do not mock, demean, or dismiss the person making them. This
                applies equally to theological disagreement, personal testimony, doubt, and
                questions that are still being worked out. Honesty and respect are not opposites;
                they are what good conversation requires of both.</li>
            <li><strong>Bring your honest questions.</strong> Doubt is not disqualifying. Seeking is
                not a sign of weakness. If you are not sure what you believe, you are in the right
                place. Ask your real questions. You do not need to perform certainty you do not
                have. At the same time, honesty runs in both directions. If you are here to argue
                in bad faith, to score points rather than to understand, this is not the space for
                that.</li>
            <li><strong>Never make doctrine a weapon.</strong> No doctrine or teaching may be used
                as a weapon against a brother or sister who names His name. We do not return insult
                for insult, exclusion for exclusion, or suspicion for suspicion.</li>
            <li><strong>Protect every person’s dignity and privacy.</strong> We protect the dignity
                of every participant. Do not share another person’s private information without
                their explicit consent. This includes contact details, personal disclosures shared
                in confidence, or identifying information about someone who has not chosen to
                identify themselves. If someone shares something vulnerable in this community,
                treat it with the care it deserves. It was offered in trust.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">What crosses the line</h3>
        <p>
            No one is required to surrender their convictions to enter this fellowship. Everyone
            is required to govern their conduct while taking part in it. Strong disagreement does
            not cross the line. These do:
        </p>
        <ol style="margin: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1.25rem; line-height: 1.9;">
            <li><strong>Harming persons.</strong> No mocking, ridicule, or abusive words toward
                anyone. (Ephesians 4:31)</li>
            <li><strong>Corrupting the search for truth.</strong> No deliberate falsehood or
                misrepresentation of another person or their tradition, and no repeating a
                misrepresentation once it has been corrected. (Ephesians 4:25)</li>
            <li><strong>Cultivating contention.</strong> No baiting, provoking, or pursuing a
                quarrel for the sake of winning. (3 Nephi 11:29)</li>
            <li><strong>Exercising dominion.</strong> No using position, credentials, pressure, or
                intimidation to force agreement or silence anyone. (Matthew 20:25-26)</li>
            <li><strong>Depriving others of a voice.</strong> No persistent interrupting,
                monopolizing, or drowning out others. (D&amp;C 88:122)</li>
            <li><strong>Trespassing on conscience.</strong> No attempt to compel another’s worship,
                confession, or conversion, and no pronouncing on another participant’s standing
                before God. (Articles of Faith 1:11)</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Absolute lines</h3>
        <p>
            Some conduct is never permitted in any gathering. It is stopped at once: the content
            comes down, and the person may be removed from that gathering. What follows depends on
            its severity, as set out under “When the agreement is broken.”
        </p>
        <ul style="margin: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1.25rem; line-height: 1.9;">
            <li><strong>Harassment, threats, or personal attacks</strong>, toward any person, for
                any reason: targeted harassment of individuals in any channel, thread or room;
                threats of any kind, explicit or implied; coordinated pressure campaigns against a
                member; content designed to humiliate rather than engage.</li>
            <li><strong>Harmful content</strong>: sexual content of any kind; hate speech targeting
                any group on the basis of race, ethnicity, religion, gender, sexual orientation,
                disability, or national origin; content that exploits, demeans, or endangers
                vulnerable people, including any content involving minors in an inappropriate
                context; graphic violence or content intended to disturb or frighten; spam, scams,
                or unsolicited promotion.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">When the agreement is broken</h3>
        <p>
            Hospitality does not require us to permit hostility. Those who persist in harassment,
            condemnation, or contempt toward others may lose the privilege of participating in our
            gatherings. Such boundaries are established not to punish belief, but to preserve the
            peace and charitable purpose of our fellowship.
        </p>
        <p>
            Violations are handled with proportionality and clarity, following the scriptural
            pattern of a first and second admonition (Titus 3:10):
        </p>
        <ul style="margin: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1.25rem; line-height: 1.9;">
            <li><strong>First offense:</strong> Direct correction from Brother Aaron, with a plain
                explanation of the violation.</li>
            <li><strong>Second offense:</strong> Final admonition. The person is told that a
                further violation results in removal.</li>
            <li><strong>Persistent violation, or a severe breach of an absolute line:</strong>
                Removal from all community spaces. Re-entry is not automatic. Reinstatement
                requires a direct appeal to Brother Aaron demonstrating a genuine shift in posture
                and a commitment to the standard of conduct this community upholds.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Whether a breach of an absolute line counts as a first offense or brings removal at
            once is Brother Aaron’s judgment, weighed by its severity.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Content that endangers a minor, and credible threats of harm,</strong> bring
            immediate removal from all community spaces and are reported to the proper
            authorities.
        </p>
        <p>
            In a live session, the host may mute or remove a participant at once to protect the
            gathering.
        </p>
        <p>
            Decisions on removal are made by Brother Aaron, and only he can lift them. The way
            back is the appeal described above.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- MODERATION -->
<section class="content-section" id="guidelines-moderation">
    <div class="content-wrapper">
        <h2>How Moderation Works</h2>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">What Gets Reviewed</h3>
        <p>
            Content submitted for public display on the website (such as community witness entries)
            passes through a moderation review before becoming visible. It is evaluated for content
            safety, theological alignment with the ministry's commitments, and pastoral sensitivity.
            Content that does not meet those criteria is declined; the author is notified using
            plain, respectful language.
            This applies only to what the ministry publishes as its own on this website. It never
            governs who may take part in our gatherings, or what anyone may say there.
        </p>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">What Happens When You Report Something</h3>
        <p>
            When a concern is reported&mdash;whether on the website or in Discord&mdash;the following
            happens:
        </p>
        <ul style="margin: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem 1.25rem; line-height: 1.9;">
            <li>The reported content is immediately hidden from public view pending review.</li>
            <li>Your report is reviewed by Brother Aaron personally. Serious concerns are treated
                as urgent and addressed without delay.</li>
            <li><strong>Your identity as a reporter is never disclosed to the person you reported.</strong>
                This is a hard policy with no exceptions.</li>
            <li>You receive an acknowledgment of your report. For urgent matters, that
                acknowledgment comes quickly.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Crisis Situations</h3>
        <p>
            If a report involves immediate danger&mdash;to the person reporting, to a third party,
            or to anyone mentioned in the content&mdash;it is treated as a critical-priority matter
            and escalated immediately. If you are in crisis right now, please reach out to
            appropriate emergency or crisis resources. This community cares about you, but it is
            not a substitute for professional crisis support.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

